<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinkel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1922</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ernst Heinkel Flugzeugwerke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinkel was a German aircraft maker founded by Ernst Heinkel at Warnemünde in 1922 and based in Rostock. It built the He 111 bomber, a mainstay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the He 178 of 1939, the first aircraft to fly on a turbojet engine. Its Rostock works were heavily bombed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>.</p></div>
